2026-09-04:

- dyn-ac (audit E10-11 redesign): MEASURED (bench/bench_ac.c, old build vs
  new linked against the same harness, hits and text hashes asserted equal on
  every row). The single dense table (1 KiB/state, 99.6% redundant slots --
  fill = edges/(states*256) = 0.004 on every bench set) is now one of two
  materializations of the same trie, chosen at build time by
  DYN_AC_DENSE_MAX (2048 states, the measured cache crossover):
    small sets stay dense  -- S10/A2/P1/L64 scan 0.97-1.05x = parity
    large sets go compact (double-array, ~31 B/state, fail-walk scan)
      M1000   6882 states:  6.8 MiB -> 180 KiB (38x), build 3.5x faster,
                            scan 1.62x / 2.65x / 1.43x FASTER (many/none/near)
      X10000 75364 states: 74.5 MiB -> 2.3 MiB (33x), build 4.4x faster,
                            scan 3.16x / 2.63x / 2.95x FASTER
    cap boundary (262144 states, the pinned 2^18 formula): 261 MiB -> 10.0 MiB
    steady (26x), cap-scan 2.98 -> 1.12 ns/B; build 57.7 -> 55.8 ms.
  Why the scan got FASTER with the memory cut: the dense table at 75k states
  is 75 MiB and the scan is cache/TLB-bound on it (25-34 MB/s); the compact
  table is ~2 MiB and the same text runs at ~100 MB/s. The root row stays
  fully materialized in both layouts, which is what bounds the compact fail
  walk (each step strictly decreases depth; total fail steps <= text length
  on ANY input, adversarial included). The 2-symbol, single-char and 60-byte
  shared-prefix adversarial sets are rows in the bench; the -1/OOM cap
  contract and the exact state-count formula in test_ac_caps.c are untouched
  and green. Differential: bench/bench_ac.c --diff (engine vs the pinned
  reference implementation in bench/ac_cand.c) is 42/42 over the systematic
  corpus, also green forced all-dense and all-compact, under ASan+UBSan;
  bench_core --hash is identical old vs new (2012816943).

2026-08-28:

- dyna:log: MEASURED (tests/bench_log_ab.js, old build vs new, controls flat
  at 26 ns empty-loop / 46 ns silent-gate). The honest table:
    short msg     643 -> 633 ns   (-1.5%)
    small fields  882 -> 894 ns   (+1.4%)
    40 fields    3168 -> 3404 ns  (+7.4%)   <-- a real regression
    err+fields    799 -> 1154 ns  (+44%)    <-- the Error-extras feature
    inline 100b   689 -> 675 ns   (-2%)
  The inline line buffer is a WASH, not the win the design assumed, and the
  reason is measured: an emit is SYSCALL-BOUND. stderr costs 630 ns/op while
  the same serialization into a buffered file costs 95 ns/op and the level
  gate alone is 30 ns, so ~600 ns of every emit is write(2). THE LEVER IS
  `buffer: true` (or a file dest), which is 6.6x. The 40-field regression is
  NOT the reserved-key check: disabling it entirely (3404 ns) and removing
  the branch (3381 ns) both leave ~7%, so it is code-layout/inlining in the
  larger translation unit, left as a known cost rather than chased.
- dyna:log: a truncated line is now CLOSED instead of severed. The cap cut
  the line at exactly 64 KiB, which for a long payload stopped mid-string
  with no closing quote, no closing brace and no newline -- emitting
  malformed JSON that also corrupted the NEXT line's framing, since a
  consumer could not find this one's end. The tail now reserves room and the
  emit path writes `...` in-band before closing, so an oversized payload
  yields one parseable line (property-tested). Fixed two JS_ToCString leaks
  in the rollover option parsers (one per Logger built with a string
  size/frequency). Verified clean under ASan+UBSan+LSan and warning-free.
- dyna:log redesign around the pino/sonic-boom, zap/zerolog and logrotate
  playbooks. Emit path is allocation-free for typical lines (512-byte inline
  line builder, heap only past it); timestamps render without snprintf (i64
  writer for epoch, cached-second ISO with manual millis); int64 round-trip
  fast path for number fields. New file destination (dest/mkdir/buffer) with
  sonic-boom-style line batching, a process-wide sink registry so Loggers on
  one path share fd + rollover state (config mismatch refused), flush(), and
  an atexit+finalizer flush belt. Rollover (size in bytes or k/m/g, frequency
  daily/hourly/ms, count retention, symlink to the active file) is lazy per
  emit, extension-last names (app.2026-08-28.1.log) that sort
  chronologically, numbering continues from the highest existing file so
  retention never causes number reuse, time-only rollover reuses the period's
  file across restarts, failed rotation falls back to stderr with a 1/s retry.
  Format integrity: caller fields colliding with frame keys (time, level,
  name, pid, hostname, msg, err) are dropped -- log forgery by field name is
  impossible; (err, fields, msg) shape added; Error extras (code, errno...)
  ride inside err; pid/hostname enrichment folded into the pre-serialized
  prefix. New human format: format:"text" renders aligned levels, name,
  message and k=v fields in one control-escaped line. Debug: node-debug
  matching (last match wins, '-' negation, '*' anywhere), multi-arg join.

2026-08-27:

- dyna:scrape modernization pass. Robots.txt (RFC 9309): group-based parser
  with product-token agent matching -- exact full-agent match beats the
  longest token match, which beats `*`; consecutive User-agent lines share
  one group; groups capped at 64 with up to 8 UA lines. Fetcher: RFC 3986
  redirect resolution (relative, dot-segment, protocol-relative, cross-host,
  each hop re-gated by robots and the SSRF check); fragment-only Location
  ends the chain instead of erroring; conditional GET against stored ETag /
  Last-Modified validators (32 entries, 1 MiB/body, 4 MiB total, FIFO) --
  a 304 returns status 304 with the cached body, fromCache and notModified
  set, counted in stats.revalidated and stats.savedBytes; caller `headers`
  merged under the crawler's identity, credential keys (Authorization,
  Cookie, Proxy-Authorization, Set-Cookie) refused at construction because
  they would leak to redirect targets; jittered exponential backoff whose
  server-provided Retry-After is never jittered. Crawl: relative linkField
  hrefs resolve against the emitting page; non-navigational schemes dropped;
  pending frontier capped at 10000. Extractor: trim, per-field default
  (satisfies optional required fields), source: true reading raw child
  source for script/style/ld+json bodies HTMLText refuses to serve as text,
  as: "url" resolving against run()'s base, as: "json" refusing invalid
  input, and ANY unknown as: string refusing at construction.
- Final honesty round: Retry-After delays BEYOND the 60s sleep cap are now
  served as the final response instead of sleeping 60s and retrying anyway
  (violating the server's instruction); the fuzz section's Fetcher forgot
  minDelayMs:0 and ran at 1 exec/s under the default 1000ms per-host floor
  (2000 runs = 33 min -- fixed, now ~90 exec/s and verified clean at 6000+
  runs; RSS growth proven to be the pre-existing per-context harness
  baseline, same or worse on fuzz_stdlib which has no scrape section);
  cache content-type strdup failure now guarded like the validators.
  New coverage: over-long Location served as final, robots redirect loop
  fail-closed, maxPages:0, Retry-After beyond cap honored-by-refusal.
- Seven-bug hardening round on dyna:scrape: (1) Retry-After HTTP-date
  deltas compared epoch-to-monotonic -- every date-form retry slept a full
  60s instead of the seconds asked; now compared against time(NULL), with a
  dt<15s regression bound so a clock mix fails LOUD. (2) ex_node_source
  leaked its chunks on the OOM path. (3) request-url fragments stripped
  before robots, the wire, and dedup keys. (4) Crawl link-following gated to
  2xx while VALUE extraction still runs on 403/500 pages -- bad pages stay
  scrapable without being trusted to point anywhere. (5) robots.txt UTF-8
  BOM no longer orphans the first group. (6) robots redirect comment/code
  aligned (2 redirects, 3 requests). (7) verified non-bugs: Cache-Control
  scan has no multi-char constants; canonical OOM-ignore is benign.
- Deeper irregularity round on dyna:scrape: Retry-After honoured as an
  IMF-fixdate HTTP-date, not just delay-seconds (the old strtod read dates
  as 0 and silently ignored them); Cache-Control: no-store excluded from
  the conditional-GET store; robots.txt redirects followed (<=3 hops, RFC
  9309 2.3.1.2) with fail-closed treatment of unsafe hops -- canonical-host
  301s no longer disallow whole sites; Crawl canonicalField (duplicate
  canonical pages emit but queue no links; no phantom-seen marking) and
  relField (nofollow skipping); Extractor default now also replaces an
  EMPTY all-array. Refcount fixes found by exit-time leak dump: value
  object leaked when a page emitted AT the depth cap (free sat inside the
  depth gate), and the pre-existing as:"number" dup-never-freed string leak
  -- both invisible to gates running ASan with detect_leaks=0; a boundary
  regression case now covers the shape. SSRF doc honesty: dyn:http's
  connect does no address-class check today, so DNS rebinding passes the
  name-shape gate; docs now say so and point at egress allowlisting.
- Page/protocol irregularity pass on dyna:scrape: https->http redirect
  downgrades refused by default (allowInsecureDowngrade opts in); declared
  Content-Length refused against maxBodyBytes BEFORE transfer (received-bytes
  cap still enforced, so lying headers stay bounded); every response carries
  the origin's Content-Type verbatim and a revalidation hit replays the
  STORED type; Crawl baseField honors a page's <base href> declaration
  (relative declarations resolve against the page; cross-host bases need
  sameHost:false); documented source-only posture -- no JS evaluation, so
  meta-refresh and SPA rendering are invisible by design.
- New hostile-page coverage: tag-soup recovery through extractor fields,
  duplicate-id multi-match, entity-encoded hrefs through as:"url", nesting
  bombs refused at HTMLParse's bound, downgrade/CL refusals, cached-type
  replay. fuzz_parsers drives Robots bodies + agents, a 30x Location and
  Extractor option strings from fuzz input; its smoke runs now seed from
  corpus_robots.
- Pre-existing gate blockers fixed en route: dyna-oauth2.c compiled with
  eight unused-variable warnings (native sanitizer builds are zero-warning
  gated) and API.md's Crawl example dereferenced p.value without an
  extractor (check-api executes all 291 blocks).

2026-08-21:

- HTTP hardening pass (dyna:net client, App, HTTPServer, HTTPServerAsync):
  header/method CRLF injection refused; hostile status lines clamp instead
  of overflowing; WS opening handshake enforces RFC 6455 4.2.1; route
  status/contentType validated at registration; prefix routes end at a
  path segment; static files served from an O_NOFOLLOW fd with HEAD and
  single-range (206/416) support; upload holds its handler by value;
  async RPC settles through a promise capability; JSON-RPC error bodies
  are escaped and bounded; notifications (no id) get no response.
- Connection lifetime: App honors Connection: close and the HTTP/1.0
  default-close, closing on the response's own send completion; requests
  pipelined behind a close go unanswered. Client rejects responses with
  both Transfer-Encoding and Content-Length.
- Resource bounds: App maxConns (default 8192); over-cap declared bodies
  answer 413; HTTPServerAsync answers 431 at the header cap; ws/sse/ws-
  client outbound queues bounded at 4 MB via dyn_aio_queued(); thread-pool
  server gains an absolute requestTimeoutMs deadline (default 30s);
  accept-error storms pause instead of spinning; io_uring SQE exhaustion
  harvests completions instead of stalling connections.
- IO: one writev per thread-pool response (+7-8% req/s vs prior tree at
  c=64/256); client threads the chunked framing flag instead of rescanning
  headers; connect poll timeout clamped; IPv6 literals bind dual-stack
  (App grows a host option).
- Tests: tests/test_http_hardening.js (94 assertions) added to the native
  suite; pentest suite updated for notification semantics.

